## Opening day

:::story
Ruwan: So, child. Seventeen weeks. Is my program ready?

Amaya: Almost, Uncle. It can remember your products, record sales, warn you about low stock and save everything to a file. But it is one huge file now. 600 lines.

Nuwan: Then let's do what real teams do. Split it into modules, each with one job, test it, and ship it. Today you stop writing exercises and start writing **software**.
:::

## Why split a program into files?

Real projects have thousands or millions of lines of code. Nobody keeps that in one file. Splitting a program into several files gives you:

- **Organisation.** Everything about products lives in one place, everything about input in another.
- **Teamwork.** Two people can work on different files at the same time without getting in each other's way.
- **Faster builds.** When you change one file, only that file needs to be recompiled.
- **Reuse.** A well-written `input.cpp` can be copied into your next project unchanged.

## Header files and source files

C++ projects usually split each module into **two** files:

| File | Extension | Contains | Think of it as |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Header file** | `.h` (or `.hpp`) | **declarations**: struct definitions, function prototypes, constants | the menu: what is available |
| **Source file** | `.cpp` | **definitions**: the actual function bodies | the kitchen: how it is made |

Other files `#include` the header to learn what exists. The linker (Chapter 2) connects the calls to the definitions.

Here is a tiny example module:

```cpp group=money file=money.h title=money.h
#ifndef MONEY_H          //@ include guard: if MONEY_H is not defined yet...
#define MONEY_H          //@ ...define it, and read the rest of the file

#include <string>

double applyVat(double amount);
std::string formatRupees(double amount);   //@ std:: in headers, never "using namespace std"

#endif                   //@ end of the include guard
```

```cpp group=money file=money.cpp title=money.cpp
#include "money.h"       //@ quotes: look in my project folder first
#include <sstream>
#include <iomanip>
using namespace std;     //@ fine inside a .cpp file

const double VAT_RATE = 0.18;   // example rate only

double applyVat(double amount) {
    return amount * (1 + VAT_RATE);
}

string formatRupees(double amount) {
    ostringstream out;
    out << "Rs. " << fixed << setprecision(2) << amount;
    return out.str();
}
```

```cpp group=money file=main.cpp run title=main.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "money.h"
using namespace std;

int main() {
    cout << formatRupees(applyVat(1000)) << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

```output
Rs. 1180.00
```

### Include guards

If two different headers both include `money.h`, the compiler could see the same struct definition twice, which is an error. The **include guard** (`#ifndef ... #define ... #endif`) makes sure the contents are only read the first time. Every header you write should have one. Many compilers also support the shorter, non-standard but very widely used `#pragma once` at the top of a header, which does the same job.

### What goes where

| Put in the header | Put in the .cpp file |
|---|---|
| struct and enum definitions | function bodies |
| function prototypes | helper functions used only in this file (mark them `static`) |
| `const` values that other files need | `using namespace std;` |
| `#include`s that the header itself needs | `#include`s needed only by the code here |

:::watch
Never put a normal function **definition** (with a body) in a header that several `.cpp` files include. Each `.cpp` file would get its own copy, and the linker would complain about a **multiple definition**. And never write `using namespace std;` in a header: every file that includes it would be forced to accept it. That is why headers write `std::string` in full.
:::

## Compiling and linking several files

Give g++ **all** the `.cpp` files. Do not list the headers; they are pulled in by `#include`.

```text
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall money.cpp main.cpp -o shop
```

Behind the scenes, each `.cpp` file is compiled separately into an **object file**, and then the linker joins them. You can do the two steps yourself, which is what build tools do for big projects:

```text
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -c money.cpp       (creates money.o)
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -c main.cpp        (creates main.o)
g++ money.o main.o -o shop              (links them into shop)
```

If you change only `main.cpp`, you only need to recompile `main.o` and link again. With hundreds of files, that saves a lot of time.

:::nodumb
Q: I get "undefined reference to formatRupees". The header is included!
A: The header only **declares** the function. The linker also needs the **definition**, which lives in `money.cpp`. You probably forgot to put `money.cpp` on the g++ command line (or in your IDE project). Including a header is not the same as compiling its source file.

Q: Do I really type all those commands for big projects?
A: No. Projects use **build tools**. A **Makefile** lists the files and how to build them, and the `make` command rebuilds only what changed. Larger projects use **CMake**, which generates build files for many systems. IDEs such as Visual Studio and CLion build multi-file projects with one click.
:::

## Debugging like a professional

So far you have debugged by reading error messages and adding `cout` lines. Those are good skills. Here are three more.

### 1. Use a debugger

A **debugger** lets you pause your program, run it one line at a time, and look at every variable while it runs. Every serious IDE has one (VS Code, Visual Studio, CLion, Code::Blocks), and on the command line there is **gdb**. First, compile with `-g` to include debugging information:

```text
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -g main.cpp -o shop
gdb ./shop
```

The most useful gdb commands:

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `break main` or `break 42` | pause when the program reaches `main`, or line 42 |
| `run` | start the program |
| `next` | run the next line (stepping over function calls) |
| `step` | run the next line, stepping **into** function calls |
| `print total` | show the value of a variable |
| `backtrace` | show the call stack (Chapter 10), very useful after a crash |
| `continue` | run until the next breakpoint |
| `quit` | leave gdb |

In an IDE, you get the same features with buttons: click in the margin to set a **breakpoint**, press the debug button, and hover over variables to see their values.

### 2. Check your assumptions with assert

`assert` (from `<cassert>`) checks a condition while the program runs. If the condition is false, the program stops immediately and tells you the file and line. It is perfect for catching "this should never happen" situations, and for small automatic tests.

```cpp run title=asserts.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cassert>
using namespace std;

double applyDiscount(double amount, double percent) {
    assert(percent >= 0 && percent <= 100);     //@ a rule the caller must follow
    return amount - amount * percent / 100;
}

int main() {
    // tiny automatic tests
    assert(applyDiscount(1000, 10) == 900);
    assert(applyDiscount(1000, 0) == 1000);
    assert(applyDiscount(500, 100) == 0);
    cout << "All tests passed." << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

```output
All tests passed.
```

If a test fails, you see something like `Assertion 'applyDiscount(1000, 0) == 1000' failed`, with the file and line number. Assertions are for catching **programmer mistakes**, not for handling bad user input: they can be switched off for release builds by defining `NDEBUG`. For user input, use the validation loops from Chapter 8.

:::industry
Professional teams write **unit tests** for their functions, using frameworks such as GoogleTest or Catch2, and run them automatically every time someone changes the code. If a change breaks an old feature, the tests catch it within minutes. Writing small, single-purpose functions (Chapter 9) is what makes testing possible.
:::

### 3. Handle errors with exceptions (a first look)

You have already met **exceptions**: `stoi("abc")` and `v.at(99)` **throw** one when something goes wrong. If nothing **catches** it, the program stops. A `try` / `catch` block lets you catch it and recover:

```cpp run title=exceptions.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string texts[3] = {"42", "abc", "7"};
    for (const string &t : texts) {
        try {
            int n = stoi(t);                       //@ may throw
            cout << t << " -> " << n << endl;
        } catch (const exception &e) {             //@ jump here if it throws
            cout << t << " -> not a number, skipped" << endl;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```output
42 -> 42
abc -> not a number, skipped
7 -> 7
```

The capstone project below uses exactly this, so that one corrupted line in a data file does not crash the whole program. Exceptions are covered properly in object-oriented programming courses.

## The capstone: Ruwan Stores Manager

Here it is: the program Amaya promised her uncle in the very first chapter. It brings together nearly everything in this book.

| Feature | Chapters used |
|---|---|
| Menu loop with validated input | 7, 8 |
| Product records | 3, 14 |
| Inventory in a vector, searching and sorting | 11, 13 |
| Case-insensitive name search | 12 |
| Functions with const references and references | 9, 10 |
| Loading and saving CSV files, appending a sales log | 16 |
| Multi-file layout with headers | 17 |

### The data file

The program keeps its products in `inventory.csv`, in the same folder as the program. Create this file before the first run (or let the program start empty and add products):

```file inventory.csv seed
code,name,price,stock
R001,Red Rice 1kg,230.50,42
O001,Coconut Oil 750ml,690.00,8
S001,Soap Bar,145.00,25
T001,Ceylon Tea 200g,520.00,4
```

### product.h and product.cpp

A `Product` struct, and two functions that convert a product to and from one line of CSV.

```cpp group=shop file=product.h title=product.h
{{FILE:product.h}}
```

```cpp group=shop file=product.cpp title=product.cpp
{{FILE:product.cpp}}
```

`catch (...)` with three dots catches **any** exception. Here it means "if either number is corrupted, report that this line could not be read".

### inventory.h and inventory.cpp

Everything that works on the whole list of products: loading, saving, finding, searching and printing.

```cpp group=shop file=inventory.h title=inventory.h
{{FILE:inventory.h}}
```

```cpp group=shop file=inventory.cpp title=inventory.cpp
{{FILE:inventory.cpp}}
```

Notice `static string lower(...)`. Here, `static` on a function means something different from Chapter 10's static local variables: the function is **private to this file**, so it cannot clash with a `lower` function anywhere else in the project.

### input.h and input.cpp

The validation loops from Chapter 8, packaged as reusable functions. Every `cin >>` is followed by `cin.ignore`, so the getline trap can never happen anywhere in the program.

```cpp group=shop file=input.h title=input.h
{{FILE:input.h}}
```

```cpp group=shop file=input.cpp title=input.cpp
{{FILE:input.cpp}}
```

If input runs out completely (end of file), the functions call `exit(1)` from `<cstdlib>` instead of looping forever.

### main.cpp

`main` is short and readable, because each menu option is its own function.

```cpp group=shop file=main.cpp run expectfile=inventory.csv expectfile=sales.csv title=main.cpp
{{FILE:main.cpp}}
```

Look at `recordSale`: `Product &p = items[i];` creates a **reference** to the product inside the vector, so `p.stock -= qty;` changes the real stock. Without the `&`, the program would change a copy and the sale would vanish.

### Build and run

```text
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra product.cpp inventory.cpp input.cpp main.cpp -o shop
./shop
```

Here is a real session. Look for the invalid quantity being rejected, and the new product being added.

```output
