:::project Make it yours
Pick at least three of these features and add them to the Ruwan Stores Manager. Each one uses skills from a specific chapter.

1. **Delete a product** by code, with a confirmation question ("Are you sure? y/n"). (Chapters 7 and 11: `erase`.)
2. **Edit a price**, rejecting prices that are zero or negative. (Chapter 8.)
3. **Daily report:** read `sales.csv` and print the number of sales, the total takings and the best-selling product. (Chapters 11 and 16.)
4. **Receipts:** when a sale is made, also write a nicely formatted receipt to a file called `receipt_<number>.txt`, using a static counter or the number of lines in `sales.csv`. (Chapters 4, 10 and 16.)
5. **Categories:** add an `enum class Category` to `Product`, store it in the CSV file as a word, and let the user list one category at a time. (Chapter 14.)
6. **Safe saving:** save to `inventory.tmp` first, and only replace `inventory.csv` if writing succeeded. (Use `std::rename` from `<cstdio>`.)
7. **Tests:** write a separate `tests.cpp` with a `main` that uses `assert` to test `toCsv`, `fromCsv`, `findByCode` and `searchByName`, and compile it together with `product.cpp` and `inventory.cpp`.
:::

## Writing code that others can read

You will spend far more of your career **reading** code than writing it. Write the code you would like to read:

- **Names that explain:** `lowStockReport`, not `f3`; `itemCount`, not `ic`.
- **Small functions** that each do one job.
- **Consistent formatting:** the same indentation, brace style and spacing everywhere. Tools like **clang-format** do this automatically.
- **Comments that explain why**, not what.
- **No magic numbers:** `const int LOW_STOCK_LIMIT = 10;` instead of a mysterious `10` in five places.
- **Compile with warnings** (`-Wall -Wextra`) and fix them all.

:::industry
Professional developers keep every version of their code in a **version control system**, almost always **Git**, hosted on services such as GitHub or GitLab. Every change is saved as a **commit** with a message explaining it, so you can see who changed what and why, and undo mistakes. Teams work on separate **branches** and merge their changes after review. Start using Git for your own projects now; your future employers will expect it.
:::

## Where to go from here

You have covered the core of programming in C++. Here is what comes next on the journey:

| Next topic | What it adds | You are ready because you know... |
|---|---|---|
| **Object-oriented programming** | classes, encapsulation, constructors, inheritance, polymorphism | structs with member functions (Chapter 14) |
| **The Standard Template Library** | `map`, `set`, `unordered_map`, `queue`, `stack`, more algorithms | vectors, iterators and `sort` (Chapters 11 and 13) |
| **Data structures and algorithms** | linked lists, trees, graphs, hashing, merge sort, quicksort, dynamic programming | pointers, recursion and Big-O (Chapters 8, 10, 13, 15) |
| **Modern C++** | move semantics, smart pointers in depth, templates, lambdas, ranges | RAII and `unique_ptr` (Chapter 15) |
| **Problem solving practice** | speed and confidence | everything in this book |

For practice, online judges such as **HackerRank**, **LeetCode** and **Codeforces** offer thousands of problems with automatic marking. Start with the easy ones, and solve at least one problem every few days. Programming is a skill like music or sport: it grows with regular practice, not with reading alone.

:::relax
Remember Chapter 1? You could not write a single line of code then. Now you can design an algorithm, write it in C++, split it into modules, test it, debug it and save its data to files. That is a real achievement. Well done.
:::

:::quiz
? What normally goes in a header (.h) file?
- Function bodies and main
* Struct definitions and function prototypes
- Only comments
- The compiled program
> Headers declare what exists; .cpp files define how it works.

? What do include guards prevent?
- Viruses
- Compiling .cpp files
* The same header being processed twice in one compilation
- Using namespace std
> #ifndef / #define / #endif makes the contents appear only once.

? You get "undefined reference to readInt" when building. What is the most likely cause?
- input.h is missing #pragma once
* input.cpp was not included in the build command
- readInt has the wrong return type in main
- The program needs more RAM
> The declaration was found, but the linker could not find the definition.

? What does compiling with -g do?
- Makes the program faster
- Turns on all warnings
* Adds debugging information so a debugger can show lines and variables
- Creates a graphical program
> -g is for debugging; -Wall is for warnings.

? What happens when assert(condition) fails?
- Nothing
- It prints a warning and continues
* The program stops and reports the failed condition with its file and line
- It throws an exception that is always caught
> assert is a check for programmer mistakes during development.
:::

:::bullets
- Split programs into modules: **headers** (`.h`) for declarations, **source files** (`.cpp`) for definitions.
- Protect every header with an **include guard** (or `#pragma once`). Never write `using namespace std;` in a header.
- Use `#include "file.h"` for your own headers and `#include <name>` for the standard library.
- Compile **all** `.cpp` files together, or compile each with `-c` and link the object files.
- "Undefined reference" usually means a missing `.cpp` file in the build.
- Debug with **breakpoints**, stepping and printing variables (compile with `-g`). Use **`assert`** to test assumptions.
- `try` / `catch` handles **exceptions** such as those thrown by `stoi` and `.at()`.
- Keep growing your project one tested feature at a time, and keep it in **Git**.
:::
